  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "An order picker in a warehouse is assigned to either Section $1$ or Section $2$ on any given day. With probability $0.65,$ the order picker works in Section $1,$ where the expected number of orders picked per day is $120.$ With probability $0.35,$ the order picker works in Section $2,$ where the expected number of orders picked per day is $95.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of orders picked per day?",
  "worked_solution": "We are asked to compute the expected number of orders picked per day. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the section to which the picker is assigned.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of orders picked, and let $Y$ be the warehouse section to which the picker is assigned.\n\n- $Y=1$ with probability $0.65,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=120.$\n\n- $Y=2$ with probability $0.35,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]=95.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]\\cdot P(Y=2) \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.65+95\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =78+33.25 \\\\  & =111.25\\end{aligned}\n$$",
